# Everything under measurements/ is a record, whatever its extension. The rules above are
# LaTeX and build noise; they have now eaten measurement files three times -- 211 .log in
# paper-io-portability, a cited build.log in paper-quic-cid-routing, and four .out files
# that a published checksum manifest listed but the tree did not contain. The first fix
# whitelisted .log alone and the next campaign wrote .out. The problem was never the
# extension: a record can carry any file type. Scope the exemption to the path instead.
# Verified: no build writes into measurements/ in this repository. A repository whose build
# DOES write under measurements/ needs a narrower form than this -- stop on it rather than
# forcing the uniform line.
#
# PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS. gitignore applies the LAST matching rule, and
# *.out sits above this line. Moving this negation up beside the *.log rule it appears to
# explain silently re-ignores every .out under measurements/ -- measured, not assumed:
# with the negation before *.out, `git check-ignore measurements/set/x.out` says IGNORED;
# after it, trackable. Keep this rule last. Test with a .out, not a .log: a .log passes
# under the broken arrangement too, which is how this got through the first time.
!measurements/**
